// A dynamic written on a lower voice whose rhythm DIFFERS from the upper voice's:
// the @f must be engraved at its OWN note's column, not at whatever the first voice
// happens to have at the same ordinal. The three older voice-dynamics books all give
// the two voices the SAME rhythm, so item index and timing agree there and the whole
// species was invisible to the corpus — this book is the one that can see it.
//
// The lower voice's second note (e2) is at timing 1/2; the upper voice's second note
// (d''8) is at timing 1/8. Reading the dynamic's item index off the STAFF's stream
// put the @f on the d''. The @staccato on the same note is the control: the script
// side already resolved the voice's own measures, so the two must agree.
title "Off-beat voice dynamics"
octave absolute
time 4/4
key c major

part rh { clef treble }

section Main {
  rh { voice { c''8 d'' e'' f'' g'' a'' b'' c''' | } { c2 e2@f@staccato | } }
}

form main { Main }

score main "voice-dynamics-offbeat" {
  staff rh
}

